	Rules
* ends_at must be later than starts_at.
* Warn on venue overlaps and lecturer clashes.
* Index offerings, enrollments, publish dates and due dates.
* course_offerings separates each semester so old data never mixes with current data.
* Use UUID keys and UTC timestamps.
10. Security
1. Row Level Security on every table. Authorization lives in the database, not in hidden buttons.
2. Students read only their enrolled courses. Lecturers write only to their assigned offerings. Admins manage everything.
3. Role and status changes are admin-only, enforced by policy and trigger.
4. Draft announcements and pending materials are invisible to students.
5. Private storage bucket with short-lived signed URLs.
6. Never ship the service-role key in the app. Only the public anon key.
7. Validate upload type (PDF), size limit (for example 10 MB) and storage path.
8. Audit log for approvals, role changes, deletions and timetable edits.
9. Store the session in encrypted storage. Clear it on logout.
10. Use synthetic data until the university approves real data.
Test both directions: write SQL tests proving permitted actions succeed and forbidden ones fail (a student editing an announcement, a lecturer editing another lecturer's course, a user promoting themselves).
11. Notifications
* Database trigger or Edge Function creates notifications rows when an announcement is published or a session changes.
* Edge Function sends FCM messages to the recipients' device tokens.
* A scheduled function sends deadline reminders.
* The in-app inbox is the source of truth. Push is only a nudge.
* Users control preferences per type (announcements, timetable, deadlines).
